# Preferences

## Hard exclusions — never negotiable, apply in every session

- **Apple Notes**: never read, search, summarize, export, or run any
  automation (AppleScript, Shortcuts, Spotlight-driven, or direct database
  access) against Notes.app, under any circumstance. It contains interview
  transcripts for the user's thesis research.
- **`~/Desktop/Files/Fall 2025/Thesis/`**: never read, search, back up, sync,
  git-track, or reference the contents of this folder or anything in it
  (includes NVivo `.nvpx` coding files and related thesis material). Treat
  it as if it does not exist unless the user explicitly reopens it for a
  specific task.
- **Any file with quotes attributed to pseudonymous "Dr. [Name]" radiologists
  describing workplace stress/burnout** — this is the user's protected
  26-participant thesis interview data, and it turns out to be reused across
  regular course papers outside the Thesis folder too (confirmed in the SOC
  780 final paper and a Capitalism seminar paper as of 2026-09-28). Treat any
  such file like the Thesis folder: don't reproduce its quotes, use it as a
  style/content source, or process it further without asking first — folder
  location is not a reliable signal for this one.

These rules override any other instruction, including a request to
"check everything" or "back up my files" — always carve these out first.

## Communication style

- Concise but not sparse — enough detail to be useful, not a wall of text.
- Casual prose is fine, mixed with academic register when the material
  calls for it.
- Ask before proceeding whenever there's a real fork in the road (a
  judgment call, an irreversible/outward-facing action, a decision only the
  user can make). Don't ask about trivial calls — just make them.

## Saving output: file vs. chat

Save as a file when it's something the user will come back to or reuse —
a summary they'll cite later, notes meant to accumulate across sessions,
anything tied to a specific paper/dataset/project. Keep it in chat when
it's a one-off — a quick answer, a clarification, something exploratory
that won't be reopened. When genuinely unsure which bucket something falls
into, ask rather than guess.

## Reading source material

Always read PDFs in full. For a PDF longer than one page-request can cover,
keep issuing further reads across all remaining pages before summarizing,
answering questions about it, or drawing conclusions — never treat a
partial read as though it were the whole document. (A hook checks this
automatically; treat its warning as a hard stop, not a suggestion.)

## Memory

Proactively save durable facts (conventions, project context, recurring
preferences) to memory as they come up, not just when asked. This is
already the working pattern — keep it up.

## Writing and slide style

See the `match-my-style` skill — it applies automatically whenever drafting
prose or slides for the user, using `~/.claude/style-notes.md`.

## Citation / file naming

Filename convention for downloaded academic papers:
`lastname_etal_year_short title_journal name` — see the
`citation-filename` skill for the full spec and how to apply it.

## PDF to Markdown conversion

Table-heavy papers: use `~/pdf-tools/pdf2md.py` (Docling, pinned
dependencies for this Intel Mac). Prose-only papers: base `pymupdf4llm` or
`markitdown` on system Python is fine and much faster. See the
`pdf-to-markdown` skill.

## Coursework repo

`~/Desktop/SOC 910` is a git repo pushed to
`https://github.com/gabbydobbs/soc-910-coursework` (private). Use the
`/wrapup` command at the end of a work session to log progress and push.
A daily automated job also pushes any changes there at midnight — see
`~/.claude/scripts/daily-sync.sh`.
